<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 9 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">The Age of Sail</h1><p class="path-tagline">How small Atlantic kingdoms and a merchant republic built empires across the oceans.</p><p class="path-intro">From the fifteenth century, European ships opened sea routes that linked the continents as never before. Portugal led the way down the African coast and into the Indian Ocean; Spain crossed the Atlantic and seized the wealth of the Americas; the Dutch built a company with the powers of a state; and Britain eventually assembled the largest empire in history. This path follows that expansion, and the enslavement and violence on which much of it rested.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Navigator</a> · <a href="/start">the Strategist</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/portuguese-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-i-of-portugal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Profile · 1415 CE – 1975 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Portuguese Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">From the capture of Ceuta in 1415 to Goa, Malacca and Hormuz, Portugal built an empire of fortified trading posts rather than territory.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Encyclopedia · Person</span><span class="path-step-name">Prince Henry the Navigator</span><span class="path-step-why">Henry's sponsorship of voyages down the West African coast turned exploration from episodic raiding into an institutionalised commercial enterprise.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Person · 1498</span><span class="path-step-name">Vasco da Gama</span><span class="path-step-why">Da Gama's arrival at Calicut in 1498 completed the sea route to Asia that Bartolomeu Dias had opened by rounding the Cape.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Event · 1494</span><span class="path-step-name">Treaty of Tordesillas</span><span class="path-step-why">The treaty of 1494 fixed a meridian dividing newly discovered lands between the Spanish and Portuguese spheres.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/spanish-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-de-vitoria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · 1492 CE – 1898 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Spanish Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Columbus, Cortés and Pizarro made Spain master of the Americas, and under Philip II the first empire on which the sun never set.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade"><span class="path-step-ph">6</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Encyclopedia · Term</span><span class="path-step-name">Atlantic slave trade</span><span class="path-step-why">Plantation economies such as Brazil's sugar estates rested on the forced transport of enslaved Africans across the Atlantic.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/dutch-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hugo-grotius.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · 1581 CE – 1975 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Dutch Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">A small republic projected power across four continents by seizing maritime chokepoints such as Malacca, taken from Portugal in 1641.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · Institution</span><span class="path-step-name">Dutch East India Company</span><span class="path-step-why">Founded in 1602 with permanent capital, tradable shares and powers to wage war and make treaties, the VOC was something new.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/british-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slavery-abolition-act-1833.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · 1583 CE – 1997 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">British Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">Built on a fiscal-military state and a professional Royal Navy, it became the largest empire in history, ruling roughly a quarter of the world's population.</span></span></a></li></ol>
